The error log indicates a test failure in `test_alarm_is_pending_invalid_id`. The test asserts that calling `rtc_alarm_is_pending()` with an invalid alarm ID (`alarms_count`) returns `-EINVAL`.

The test fails because on some platforms where `CONFIG_RTC_ALARM` is enabled, the underlying RTC device may not support alarms. In such cases, the `alarms-count` property in the devicetree is typically 0.

When `alarms_count` is 0, the test calls `rtc_alarm_is_pending(rtc, 0)`. Since there are no alarms, ID 0 is invalid. However, if the driver does not implement the alarm functionality at all, the RTC API wrapper returns `-ENOSYS` (function not implemented) instead of `-EINVAL` (invalid argument). This causes the assertion to fail.

The other alarm-related tests in this suite, `test_alarm` and `test_alarm_callback`, contain loops that iterate up to `alarms_count`. If `alarms_count` is 0, these loops do not execute, and the tests pass vacuously.

To make `test_alarm_is_pending_invalid_id` consistent and robust, it should also handle the case where there are no alarms. The appropriate action is to skip the test, as it is not meaningful to test for an out-of-bounds alarm ID when no alarms are supported.

The fix is to add a check at the beginning of `test_alarm_is_pending_invalid_id`. If `alarms_count` is 0, the test will be skipped.

